¬}\pard \s43 \li1512 \fi-503 \ri1007 \ql \tx1512 \tx8265 trackMouse:inRect:ofView:untilMouseUp:\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b trackMouse:\b0 (NSEvent\'80*)\i theEvent\
\fi0 \b \i0 inRect:\b0 (NSRect)\i cellFrame\
\fi0 \b \i0 ofView:\b0 (NSView\'80*)\i aTextView\
\fi0 \b \i0 untilMouseUp:\b0 (BOOL)\i flag\
\fs14\
\i0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Handles a mouse-down event on the receiver\'27s image. \i theEvent\i0  is the mouse-down event. \i cellFrame\i0  is the region of \i aTextView\i0  in which further mouse events should be tracked. \i aTextView\i0  is the view which received the event. It\'27s assumed to be an NSTextView, and should be the focus view. If \i flag\i0  is YES, the receiver tracks the mouse until a mouse-up event occurs; if \i flag\i0  is NO, it stops tracking when a mouse-dragged event occurs outside of \i cellFrame\i0 . Returns YES if the receiver successfully finished tracking the mouse (typically through a mouse-up event), NO otherwise (such as when the mouse is dragged outside \i cellFrame\i0 ).\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSTextAttachmentCell\'27s implementation of this method calls upon \i aTextView\i0 \'27s delegate to handle the event. If \i theEvent\i0  is a mouse-up event for a double click, the text attachment cell sends the delegate a \b textView:doubleClickedOnCell:inRect:\b0  message and returns YES. Otherwise, depending on whether the user clicks or drags the cell, it sends the delegate a \b textView:clickedOnCell:inRect:\b0  or a \b textView:draggingCell:inRect:event:\b0  message and returns YES. NSTextAttachmentCell\'27s implementation returns NO only if \i flag\i0  is NO and the mouse is dragged outside of \i cellFrame\i0 . The delegate methods are invoked only if the delegate responds.\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b wantsToTrackMouse\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b trackMouse:inRect:ofView:untilMouseUp:\b0 \'80(NSCell), \f3 -\f1 \'80\b lockFocus\b0 \'80(NSView)\
\fs14\
\fs46\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s43 \li1512 \fi-503 \ri1007 \ql \tx1512 \tx8265 \f5 \b0 \fs52 p\tab \f0 \b \fs28 {{\NeXTHelpMarker9422 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_method:[NSTextAttachmentCell-wantsToTrackMouse];}
¬}\pard \s43 \li1512 \fi-503 \ri1007 \ql \tx1512 \tx8265 wantsToTrackMouse\
\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80(BOOL)\b wantsToTrackMouse\
\fs14\
\b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Returns YES if the receiver will handle a mouse event occurring over its image (to support dragging, for example), NO otherwise. NSTextAttachmentCell\'27s implementation of this method returns YES. The NSView containing the cell should invoke this method before sending a \b trackMouse:inRect:ofView:untilMouseUp:\b0  message.\
\fs14\
\fs28 For an attachment in an attributed string, if the attachment cell returns NO its attachment character should be selected rather than the cell being asked to track the mouse. this results in the attachment icon behaving as any regular glyph in text.\
\fs14\
